> > > This seems to work for me, if I religiously set context, input, and output for all models, on any context length change to the model being used.
> > 
> > 
> > How have you been doing this [@lee-b](https://github.com/lee-b) ? It wasn't clear to me.
> 
> In `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`, create a provider section like this:
> 
> ```
>   "provider": {
>     "my-server": {
>       "name": "my-server",
>       "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
>       "options": {
>         "apiKey": "sk-some-key",
>         "baseURL": "http://my.server:myport/v1",
>         "includeUsage": false
>       },
>       "models": {
>         "my-model": {
>           "limit": {
>             "context": 65536,
>             "input": 47014,
>             "output": 18432
>           },
>           "modalities": {
>             "input": [
>               "text",
>               "image"
>             ],
>             "output": [
>               "text"
>             ]
>           },
>           "reasoning": false,
>           "tool_call": false,
>           "variants": {
>             "reasoning": {},
>             "standard": {}
>           }
>         }
>       }
>     }
>   }
> ```
> 
> Change `baseURL` and `apiKey` to suit your server, of course, and `my-model` to match the model name that your server lists if you `curl ../v1/models` on it.
> 
> Then it've just between tweaking the `context` to suit whichever model I have configured on the server, and subtracting `input` from that to set the `output` value too.
> 
> `input` should really come from the model docs, if the docs tell you -- if it's too small, the model may be cut off before it does a tool call, interrupting the agency/autonomy.
> 
> Remove `, "image"` from `modalities` if your model can't do vision.
> 
> I'm not sure about the `reasoning`/`tool_call`/`variants` part, but this basic `provider` config section works for me, with multiple models (qwen3.5, minimax, step-3.5-flash, etc.).

@lee-b I tested your approach and it worked very well. Of course, it still doesn't completely eliminate the problem, but it greatly improved usability. My example:

```json
...
  "provider": {    
    "openai": {
      "models": {
        "gpt-5.2": {
          "limit": {
            "context": 400000,
            "input": 250000,
            "output": 128000
          },
          "fast": {
            "disabled": true
          },
          "include": ["reasoning.encrypted_content"]
        },
        "gpt-5.2-codex": {
          "limit": {
            "context": 400000,
            "input": 250000,
            "output": 128000
          },
          "fast": {
            "disabled": true
          },
          "include": ["reasoning.encrypted_content"]
        },
...
```

I too encountered a terrible amount of friction, trying every conceivable combination of keys and values making incremental progress but eventually getting stuck on a series of "Resource not found" errors.  This was all after registering the Azure Cognitive Services (A.K.A. AI Foundry) provider API key, which now appears in my `auth.json`. I also set the `AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY` env var. 

In Foundry this was the endpoint for the one model:
https://XXXX.cognitiveservices.azure.com/openai/deployments/XXX_gpt-4.1-mini/chat/completions?api-version=2025-01-01-preview

I initially tried using the Desktop and CLI to update things, but eventually ended up poking at the `opencode.json` directly.  This is the section which ultimately worked, with some redaction:

```json
  ..
  "provider": {
    "foundry": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/azure",
      "options": {
        "name": "foundry",
        "apiKey": "{env:AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY}",
        "baseURL": "https://XXXX.cognitiveservices.azure.com/openai/",
        "apiVersion": "2025-01-01-preview",
        "useDeploymentBasedUrls": true
      },
      "models": {
        "XXX_gpt-4.1-mini": { "name": "gpt-4.1-mini" }
      }
    }
  },
  ..
```

This was several hours in the making, but I now have a working connection to the model in both the Desktop and CLI.
